:claimreview_3a19af0d618d905e8eea349db30dfc81 a schema:Claim,
        schema:ClaimReview ;
    rdfs:seeAlso :claimreview_9ed3c51cc0ea77255831222050277ff3 ;
    schema:about :0 ;
    schema:associatedClaimReview :claimreview_9ed3c51cc0ea77255831222050277ff3 ;
    schema:author :person_17f931e83627dc415846118ddfa81626 ;
    schema:citation "http://icecap.us/images/uploads/MM.JGR07-background.pdf"^^schema:URL ;
    schema:claimReviewed :claimreview_3a19af0d618d905e8eea349db30dfc81 ;
    schema:dateCreated "2015-07-05T00:00:00"^^schema:Date ;
    schema:description "While urban areas are undoubtedly warmer than surrounding rural areas, this has had little to no impact on warming trends."@en ;
    schema:headline "Climate Myth..."@en ;
    schema:inLanguage "English"^^schema:Text ;
    schema:keywords "CO2 emissions"@en,
        "climate change"@en,
        "global warming"@en,
        "skepticism"@en,
        "skeptics"@en ;
    schema:license "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/"^^schema:URL ;
    schema:name "Does Urban Heat Island effect exaggerate global warming trends?"@en ;
    schema:publisher :organization_sks ;
    schema:reviewBody """The Urban Heat Island Effect (UHI) is a phenomenon whereby the concentration of structures and waste heat from human activity (most notably air conditioners and internal combustion engines) results in a slightly warmer envelope of air over urbanised areas when compared to surrounding rural areas. It has been suggested that UHI has significantly influenced temperature records over the 20th century with rapid growth of urban environments.
Scientists have been very careful to ensure that UHI is not influencing the temperature trends. To address this concern, they have compared the data from remote stations (sites that are nowhere near human activity) to more urban sites. Likewise, investigators have also looked at sites across rural and urban China, which has experienced rapid growth in urbanisation over the past 30 years and is therefore very likely to show UHI. The difference between ideal rural sites compared to urban sites in temperature trends has been very small:
Another way to explore the UHI would be to look at where the majority of warming has occurred across the globe. The UHI should match where most people live. However, if you look at the 2006 global temperature anomaly (figure 2.), you find that the greatest difference in temperatures for the long term averages where across Russia, Alaska, far north Canada and Greenland and not where major urbanisation has occurred.
The Urban Heat Effect has no significant influence on the record of global temperature trends.
Basic rebuttal written by mothincarnate
Update July 2015:
Here is a related lecture-video from Denial101x - Making Sense of Climate Science Denial"""@en ;
    schema:reviewRating [ a schema:Rating ;
            schema:bestRating "1"^^schema:Number ;
            schema:name "False"^^schema:Text ;
            schema:ratingExplanation "Climate Myth..."@en ;
            schema:ratingValue "0"^^schema:Number ;
            schema:worstRating "0"^^schema:Number ] ;
    schema:text """It's Urban Heat Island effect
A paper by Ross McKitrick, an economics professor at the  University of Guelph, and Patrick Michaels, an environmental studies  professor at the University of Virginia, concludes that half of the  global warming trend from 1980 to 2002 is caused by Urban Heat Island. (McKitrick & Michaels)"""@en,
        """The Urban Heat Island Effect (UHI) is a phenomenon whereby the concentration of structures and waste heat from human activity (most notably air conditioners and internal combustion engines) results in a slightly warmer envelope of air over urbanised areas when compared to surrounding rural areas. It has been suggested that UHI has significantly influenced temperature records over the 20th century with rapid growth of urban environments.
Scientists have been very careful to ensure that UHI is not influencing the temperature trends. To address this concern, they have compared the data from remote stations (sites that are nowhere near human activity) to more urban sites. Likewise, investigators have also looked at sites across rural and urban China, which has experienced rapid growth in urbanisation over the past 30 years and is therefore very likely to show UHI. The difference between ideal rural sites compared to urban sites in temperature trends has been very small:
Another way to explore the UHI would be to look at where the majority of warming has occurred across the globe. The UHI should match where most people live. However, if you look at the 2006 global temperature anomaly (figure 2.), you find that the greatest difference in temperatures for the long term averages where across Russia, Alaska, far north Canada and Greenland and not where major urbanisation has occurred.
The Urban Heat Effect has no significant influence on the record of global temperature trends.
Basic rebuttal written by mothincarnate
Update July 2015:
Here is a related lecture-video from Denial101x - Making Sense of Climate Science Denial"""@en ;
    schema:url "https://skepticalscience.com/urban-heat-island-effect-basic.htm"^^schema:URL .

:claimreview_3d63391960c6076ccd4d1a97dfbe727c a schema:Claim,
